X('incoterms-2020-explained',[
 ('How to choose a term',['Start with who you trust to organise the transport and who is better placed to do it. A buyer with its own forwarder and a good rate may prefer EXW, FCA or FOB. A seller who wants control of the freight may sell CIF, CIP or DAP. Then check where the risk passes, because that point decides who bears loss or damage on the way.','The terms are about delivery, risk and costs. They do not transfer ownership of the goods, and they do not say anything about payment.']),
 ('A comparison of risk points',[('table',['Term','Risk passes to buyer','Mode'],[['EXW','At the seller\'s premises','Any'],['FCA','When handed to the buyer\'s carrier at the named place','Any'],['FOB','When the goods are on board at the port of shipment','Sea only'],['CIF','When the goods are on board at the port of shipment','Sea only'],['DAP','On arrival, ready for unloading','Any'],['DDP','On arrival, with import duty paid by the seller','Any']],'Selected terms only. Incoterms 2020 has eleven terms in all.')]),
 ('Common errors',['Using FOB or CIF for containers handed over at an inland depot. FCA or CIP suit that better.','Writing only the term and not the place, such as "FOB" alone instead of "FOB Mundra".','Assuming CIF means the seller covers all insurance. It requires only minimum cover unless agreed otherwise.'])],
 [('Do Incoterms transfer ownership of the goods?','No. They set delivery, risk and cost between the buyer and the seller. Ownership is decided by the sales contract and the governing law.')])
X('fob-incoterm-explained',[
 ('A typical Indian export',['An Indian exporter sells under FOB Nhava Sheva. The exporter delivers the goods to the port, clears them for export and loads them on the vessel nominated by the buyer. When the goods are on board, the risk passes. The buyer pays the ocean freight and insurance and handles the import clearance and delivery at the destination.','The buyer\'s forwarder usually collects the bill of lading and the exporter sends the documents for payment.']),
 ('Points to agree in advance',['Who books the vessel and when the nominated ship will arrive. The seller needs a date to plan the cargo delivery.','The cut-off times at the port, and who pays for any delay or rolled cargo.','Who pays the terminal handling charges at origin, because practice differs and it can cause a dispute.']),
 ('Why it is not ideal for containers',['For containerised cargo, the goods are normally handed to the line at a terminal or an inland depot long before they are loaded on the ship. The seller then bears the risk between the handover and loading, although it has no control over the cargo. FCA reflects the real delivery point better for containers.'])],
 [('Who pays the origin charges under FOB?','The seller typically pays the costs up to loading on the vessel, including export clearance. Terminal handling practice varies, so confirm it in the contract.')])
X('cif-incoterm-explained',[
 ('What the seller must arrange',['The seller books and pays the carriage to the named port of destination, buys marine insurance for the buyer\'s benefit and clears the goods for export. The risk passes to the buyer at loading on the vessel at the origin port, even though the seller paid for the freight. If the cargo is lost at sea, the buyer claims on the insurance policy that the seller bought.','The minimum insurance under the 2020 rules is the lower level of cover, so a buyer who wants fuller cover should say so in the contract or buy its own policy.']),
 ('How it feeds the import duty',['For an Indian importer on a CIF invoice, the freight and insurance are already in the price. The assessable value is then close to the invoice value, with only a few additions such as landing charges where applicable. On an FOB invoice, the importer adds the freight and insurance to reach the same figure.']),
 ('Tips',['Name the destination port clearly, with the terminal if needed.','Ask the seller for a copy of the insurance certificate and the policy terms.','Remember that the seller chooses the carrier and the sailing, so ask for the vessel details if timing matters to you.'])],
 [('Does the CIF seller bear the risk until the destination?','No. The seller pays for freight and insurance to the destination, but the risk passes to the buyer when the goods are on board at the port of shipment.')])
X('exw-ex-works-incoterm',[
 ('What the buyer takes on',['Under EXW, the buyer arranges the pick-up at the seller\'s premises, loads the goods, handles export clearance in the origin country and pays all the costs and risks from that point. The seller only has to make the goods available.','If the buyer is a foreign company with no presence in India, the export clearance can be very hard, because the exporter of record needs an IEC and a GST position.']),
 ('Why exporters prefer FCA',['An Indian seller usually wants to control the export clearance and the shipping bill, since benefits such as drawback and RODTEP and the GST refund depend on the seller being the exporter. FCA lets the seller clear the goods for export and hand them to the buyer\'s carrier, which keeps these benefits with the seller.']),
 ('Where EXW does work',['Domestic sales between businesses in the same country.','A buyer with a strong local team that handles the pick-up and clearance and has agreed to it with the seller.','Sample shipments with a courier, where the buyer sends its own account.'])],
 [('Is EXW good for Indian exporters?','Usually not for export. The seller loses control of the export clearance and the benefits that depend on it. FCA or FOB is more common.')])
X('fca-free-carrier-incoterm',[
 ('The place of delivery decides the risk',['Under FCA, the seller delivers the goods to the buyer\'s carrier at a named place. If the place is the seller\'s premises, the seller must load the goods on to the buyer\'s vehicle. If it is any other place, such as a depot or a terminal, the seller delivers the goods on its own vehicle, ready for unloading, and the buyer\'s carrier unloads.','The seller handles the export clearance in either case, which is why it is a popular term for Indian exporters.']),
 ('The on-board bill of lading option',['A buyer who has a letter of credit calling for an on-board bill of lading can ask the seller, in the contract, to obtain one from the carrier. The Incoterms 2020 rules include this option. The carrier issues the bill after the goods are loaded, and the seller passes it to the buyer, but the risk still moved at the earlier handover.']),
 ('Tips',['Name the exact place of delivery, such as an ICD or a CFS, to avoid a dispute about who loads and unloads.','Confirm how the cargo will be moved to the port, and who will hold the documents.','Agree the timing for the buyer\'s carrier to collect, so the seller\'s risk does not go on for longer than planned.'])],
 [('Is FCA better than FOB for containers?','Often yes. For containers handed over before loading, FCA puts the delivery at the real handover point, so the risk is allocated more sensibly.')])
X('dap-delivered-at-place-incoterm',[
 ('What the seller covers',['The seller organises and pays for the carriage to the named place in the buyer\'s country, bears the risk until the goods arrive, ready for unloading, and handles the export clearance. The buyer unloads, clears the goods for import and pays the import duty and taxes.','The seller has no control over the import formalities, so delays at the buyer\'s customs are the buyer\'s concern.']),
 ('Why it is popular with Indian exporters',['It lets the exporter offer the buyer a delivered price and keep control of the freight, without the burden of import duty in a foreign country. It is simpler than DDP where the seller has no way of paying import taxes abroad.']),
 ('Points to agree',['The exact delivery address, because the seller bears the cost and the risk up to that place.','The unloading arrangements: the seller does not unload, but it must give the buyer the means to do it.','Who deals with the charges or delays at the buyer\'s port or border that are not in the seller\'s control, such as demurrage.'])],
 [('Who pays the import duty under DAP?','The buyer. The seller delivers to the named place, but the import clearance and duty are the buyer\'s, unlike DDP.')])
X('ddp-delivered-duty-paid-incoterm',[
 ('What it takes to run DDP',['The seller bears all the costs and risks up to the destination, including the import clearance, the duty and the taxes in the buyer\'s country. To do this, the seller usually needs an importer of record or a local agent in the destination country, and an understanding of that country\'s rules and taxes.','The price must include everything, so the seller should get a reliable duty quote before it commits.']),
 ('Where it goes wrong',['A change in duty or a new tax after the contract is signed can reduce the seller\'s margin.','Customs in the destination country may reject the value, the origin or the classification, with fines that the seller must pay.','Some countries do not allow a foreign seller to be the importer, so the term cannot be used there.']),
 ('When it works',['Small, regular shipments to a market where you already have a local partner.','Courier and e-commerce shipments, where the carrier collects the duty on delivery under a prepaid arrangement.','Large buyers who ask for a delivered price and where the seller has the logistics to handle it.'])],
 [('Is DDP risky for the seller?','It can be, because the seller carries the duty, the taxes and the clearance risk in the buyer\'s country. Use it only where you know the rules and can control the costs.')])
X('cpt-and-cip-incoterms',[
 ('Where each is used',['CPT and CIP are the terms for multimodal and container transport, where the goods are handed to a carrier at an inland point and the carriage continues by sea, road or rail. The seller delivers the goods to the first carrier, and the risk passes to the buyer at that moment, while the seller pays for the carriage to the named destination.','Under CIP, the seller also buys insurance for the buyer, and under the 2020 rules this is the higher level of cover, which is a difference from CIF.']),
 ('How they compare with CFR and CIF',['CFR and CIF are for sea freight, with the risk passing on board the vessel. CPT and CIP apply to any mode, and the risk passes when the goods are given to the first carrier. For containers, CPT and CIP match the real handover at an inland depot much better.']),
 ('Planning notes',['Name the destination place, and not only the port, so that the seller\'s cost is clear.','Check who handles the unloading at destination and any charges there, which usually go to the buyer.','For CIP, ask for the insurance certificate and check that the cover and the insured party suit you.'])],
 [('What is the difference between CIP and CIF?','CIP applies to any mode of transport and passes the risk when goods are given to the first carrier, while CIF is for sea and passes the risk on board. CIP also needs a higher level of insurance cover under Incoterms 2020.')])
X('fob-vs-cif-which-to-choose',[
 ('Cost and control',['FOB gives the buyer control of the freight and insurance, which suits a buyer with a good forwarder, a regular volume and its own insurance. CIF gives the seller control, which suits a buyer with no logistics team or a small shipment. Whoever controls the freight also controls the choice of carrier, the sailing and the claims process.','Price comparison is not enough. Compare the total cost to the same point, and not only the headline rates.']),
 ('A numerical comparison',['Suppose a supplier quotes US$ 10,000 FOB and US$ 10,900 CIF. The CIF price includes about US$ 900 for freight and insurance. If your own forwarder charges US$ 700 for freight and US$ 60 for insurance, the FOB route costs US$ 10,760 and is cheaper by US$ 140. If your forwarder is more expensive than the supplier\'s, CIF wins. The numbers in your case will differ, so compare both quotes on the same basis.']),
 ('How it affects duty and claims',['For customs value, freight and insurance are added to an FOB price, so the assessable value is about the same either way. In a claim, FOB means you deal with the carrier and your own insurer. With CIF you rely on the seller\'s policy, which may have lower cover and be slower to claim against.'])],
 [('Which is better for a small importer, FOB or CIF?','CIF is often simpler, because the seller arranges freight and insurance. FOB can be cheaper and gives more control if you have a good forwarder. Compare the total landed cost before you decide.')])
